<?nvs
// The receiving end of an upload, as a program.
//
// [ADR 0105](/docs/decisions/0105.md)
// states one rule, and this fixture is the shape of it: **there is no temp
// file, because the destination is the application's own call.** The host never
// picks a path, never writes one behind the program's back, and never leaves
// one behind for a later request to find -- which is also why the last line of
// this program is the one that proves the directory is gone.
//
// What a *program* can show of that is the shape and the members, not a body:
// nothing is inbound here, so the walk refuses rather than yielding parts. The
// end-to-end half -- a real multipart body, its two parts, and a large one
// received at bounded resident memory against a high-water mark -- is
// `crates/nvs-server`'s own suite, which m7.md names as ADR 0105's fixture
// list. This leg is the surface a handler writes, run to the point where the
// absence of a request stops it.
//
// The grant this needs is in `nvs.toml`, keyed on this entry, for the reason
// `examples/files.nvs`'s block gives: `Core\IO::temporaryDir()` answers with the
// host's own path, and that is not a name this repository can write down.

// A directory this program owns, so the fixture leaves nothing behind.
string $dir = Core\IO::temporaryDir();

// The whole receiving end, written exactly as a request handler writes it: the
// walk, the launderer, and one of § 3's two consumers. A part is received once
// and in one of two ways -- into a bounded buffer with `readAll({max})`, or
// straight to its destination with `saveTo`, which is § 4's delegation to
// `Core\IO::writeStream` and never holds more than a chunk.
//
// It refuses here, and the refusal is this leg's assertion. `Core\Request` has
// nothing to read because nothing arrived (ADR 0012 § 7), and a program that
// found an upload anyway would have found it in exactly the place ADR 0105
// removed: ambient state the host left lying about.
try {
    foreach (Core\Request::files() as Core\Request\Part $part) {
        string $dest = Core\IO::within($dir, 'upload.bin');
        $part->saveTo($dest, { max: 4096, overwrite: true });
        echo 'saved ', Core\IO::size($dest), " bytes\n";
    }
    echo "a program answered a request\n";
} catch (LogicError $none) {
    echo 'no request', "\n";
}

// And the member `saveTo` delegates to, which a program *can* run: the chunks
// reach disk one at a time, so what the writer holds is the largest single
// chunk rather than the length of the stream. That is the property m7 asserts
// under a real body; here it is the same call with the source in hand.
array<bytes> $chunks = [];
int $i = 0;
while ($i < 1024) {
    $chunks[] = '0123456789' as bytes;
    $i = $i + 1;
}
string $blob = Core\Path::join($dir, 'payload.bin');
Core\IO::writeStream($blob, $chunks, { overwrite: true });
echo 'streamed ', Core\IO::size($blob), " bytes\n";

// The destination is a name this program built with `Core\Path::join` from a
// directory it owns -- never one derived from what arrived. ADR 0105 leaves the
// filename a part *claims* as `tainted` text, so `Core\IO::within` is the
// launderer standing between it and a path, exactly as `examples/files.nvs`
// shows it and exactly as the walk above spells it.
try {
    string $escape = Core\IO::within($dir, '../../etc/passwd');
    echo "a claimed filename escaped the destination\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo 'escape refused', "\n";
}

// And back to nothing. No temp file outlives the program, because none was ever
// made on its behalf.
Core\IO::remove($blob);
Core\IO::removeDir($dir);
echo 'removed', "\n";
